            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 1,
          "low": 0,
          "param": "rounding"
        },
        {
          "choices": null,
          "high": 16,
          "low": 14,
          "param": "data_width"
        },
        {
          "choices": null,
          "high": 16,
          "low": 14,
          "param": "n_iter"
        },
        {
          "choices": null,
          "high": 4,
          "low": 2,
          "param": "m"
        }
      ],
      "why": "pipelined_m registers every m stages, so it has fewer flip-flops and less area than fully pipelined at the same n_iter, while still producing 1 result/cycle (so it keeps the 32 Msps throughput). The cost is a longer combinational path per stage (m micro-rotations), which lowers Fmax and can jeopardise the 0.5 us batch deadline. I keep m in 2..4 so the path stays short enough to still reach ~64 MHz, and let the search trade the area savings against the Fmax/latency risk. This family is where the cheapest-area points on the front are likely to live, so it is worth a meaningful share."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec has three hard gates: (1) throughput >= 32 Msps, (2) max_abs_err <= 2^-12, and (3) the system batch deadline sys_p99_batch_us <= 0.5 us. Only the pipelined families produce 1 result/cycle, so they are the only ones that can clear the throughput gate; iterative/unrolled_k are excluded anyway by my fleet assignment. The batch deadline is the binding constraint: 32 requests arrive at once and all 32 results must return within 0.5 us, i.e. 32 cycles in 0.5 us, requiring Fmax >= 64 MHz. That forces a short critical path, which in turn caps n_iter and angle_guard. The accuracy gate (2^-12) then pins data_width, n_iter, angle_guard, frac_guard and rounding to a narrow feasible band, so I bound data_width and n_iter to 14..16 and angle_guard to -2..2 rather than sweeping the full registry ranges. I split the budget between pipelined (0.55) and pipelined_m (0.45): pipelined is the safe, high-Fmax anchor that is most likely to satisfy all three gates and define the reliable part of the front, while pipelined_m (m=2..4) is where the lowest-area points are expected to sit, so it gets a substantial share to map the area/latency trade-off. The selection rule is min area, but hypervolume rewards covering the whole accuracy-vs-area curve, so I keep the accuracy-relevant parameters (angle_guard, frac_guard, rounding) varied across both families rather than collapsing to a single accuracy point."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "rationale": "The front is feasible and well-characterized for the selection rule (min area). The cheapest feasible design (pipelined, W=16, N=14, ag=2, fg=2, round) has area 39385 um^2, throughput 143 MSPS (>> 32), max_abs_err 0.000204 (<= 0.000244), and sys_p99_batch 0.28 us (<= 0.5 us). The front spans the accuracy trade-off (12.3..12.9 bits) at the minimum-area corner, which is what the selection rule picks. pipelined_m yielded no feasible designs (best accuracy 11.97 < 12.0 required), and iterative/unrolled_k cannot meet the 32 MSPS throughput constraint (they produce one result every several cycles, so their throughput is far below the pipelined 143 MSPS). Since this is the final round, I stop and let the L2 simulation re-select among the feasible front designs."
,
